      title: "Patent 568,176: The Resonant Transformer",
      content: `On **September 22, 1896**, the United States Patent Office issued Tesla **Patent 568,176**, titled *"Apparatus for Producing Electric Currents of High Frequency and Potential."* The drawings show an unfamiliar device: not a conventional iron-core transformer but a pair of loosely coupled **air-core coils**, one short and fat, one long and thin, both connected to capacitors and a sputtering spark gap.

This was the **Tesla coil** in its classical form. By the time Tesla demonstrated it at the **1893 Chicago World's Fair**, he was using the device to throw 18-foot arcs from a hand-held electrode, light wireless tubes across a darkened auditorium, and famously pass alternating current through his own body to a roomful of stunned electricians.

The coil is **not a magic-trick device**. Every parameter has an engineering reason. It is a **resonant air-core transformer** that exploits two physical principles simultaneously: **mutual inductance** between two coils with a deliberately weak magnetic coupling (k ≈ 0.1 to 0.2), and **LC resonance** in both the primary and secondary circuits tuned to the same frequency — typically **50 kHz to 500 kHz**.

A wall-outlet **120V or 240V supply** drives a step-up transformer that charges the primary capacitor to perhaps **15,000 volts**. A spark gap fires. An oscillating current rings in the primary tank circuit. That oscillation couples weakly into a much larger secondary coil where successive resonant cycles **pump energy into the secondary capacitance** — the small self-capacitance of the coil itself plus a metal toroid mounted on top.

Output: **300,000 to several million volts** of high-frequency RF at a few hundred milliamps. Spectacular streamers. Almost no heat. And a device whose physics has been continuously elaborated for 130 years.`,
